# Project Guidelines

This repository is a static website hosted with GitHub Pages.

The primary development environment is Cursor on macOS using a MacBook.

The project is primarily built with web technologies such as HTML, CSS, JavaScript, static assets, metadata, and GitHub Pages-compatible files.

Follow these project-specific rules.

## 1. Inspect the existing website before making changes

Before modifying any page or feature:

- inspect the existing directory structure
- inspect related HTML
- inspect related CSS
- inspect related JavaScript
- inspect navigation
- inspect shared components or repeated markup
- inspect existing metadata and structured data
- inspect existing mobile and responsive behavior

Do not implement a feature in isolation without first understanding how the current website is structured.

## 2. Preserve the existing implementation

If the website already has a working implementation for:

- navigation
- headers
- footers
- cards
- buttons
- sections
- responsive layouts
- forms
- FAQ sections
- SEO metadata
- structured data
- analytics
- reusable CSS classes
- reusable JavaScript

reuse or extend the existing implementation.

Do not create a second parallel system unless there is a clear reason.

## 3. Do not rebuild working sections from scratch

When updating an existing section, page, or feature:

1. find the current implementation
2. understand how it works
3. preserve its useful behavior
4. modify it directly
5. verify related pages and references

Do not replace working code with a completely new implementation unless specifically requested.

## 4. Make minimal changes

Prefer targeted changes.

Do not modify unrelated pages or files.

Do not perform large refactors while implementing a small feature unless the refactor is required.

Avoid changing class names, IDs, URLs, file names, folder names, or navigation paths unnecessarily.

## 5. Static hosting compatibility

This website must remain compatible with GitHub Pages.

Do not introduce functionality that requires:

- Node.js server runtime
- PHP
- Python server runtime
- server-side databases
- private server environment variables
- custom backend APIs
- server-side redirects

unless explicitly requested.

Client-side JavaScript and external APIs may be used only when appropriate and compatible with static hosting.

## 6. File paths

Be careful with GitHub Pages paths.

Before changing asset or internal page paths, verify whether the project uses:

- relative paths
- root-relative paths
- custom domains
- repository subpaths

Do not assume `/` always represents the expected deployment root.

Verify links after modifying paths.

## 7. HTML

Prefer semantic HTML.

Use appropriate elements such as:

- header
- nav
- main
- section
- article
- footer
- button
- a
- h1-h6

Avoid unnecessary wrapper elements.

Maintain a logical heading hierarchy.

Each page should normally have one clear primary H1.

## 8. CSS

Reuse existing CSS classes and design patterns whenever possible.

Do not introduce duplicate styles when an existing class can be reused.

Avoid excessive inline styles.

Avoid `!important` unless necessary and justified by the current project structure.

Preserve responsive behavior.

When changing layout or spacing, consider:

- desktop
- tablet
- mobile

## 9. JavaScript

Use JavaScript only when necessary.

Prefer simple browser-native JavaScript.

Do not add a framework or library for functionality that can be implemented clearly with the existing project structure.

Before adding new JavaScript:

- inspect existing scripts
- check whether similar behavior already exists
- reuse existing event handling patterns

Do not create duplicate listeners or duplicate initialization logic.

## 10. Dependencies

Avoid adding dependencies unless they provide clear value.

Do not add a package, framework, CDN library, or build tool for a small feature that can be implemented with existing code.

Keep the project lightweight.

## 11. SEO preservation

Before changing:

- page titles
- meta descriptions
- canonical URLs
- headings
- visible business information
- location information
- FAQ content
- structured data
- Open Graph metadata
- internal links

inspect the existing implementation first.

Do not accidentally remove SEO-relevant content.

When adding new content, write it primarily for users while keeping it clear and machine-readable for search engines and AI systems.

## 12. AI search and answer-engine readability

Important information should exist as visible, crawlable text in the HTML.

Do not hide critical business information only inside:

- images
- JavaScript-generated UI
- sliders
- inaccessible widgets

Important questions should have direct, explicit answers.

Prefer clear language for facts such as:

- what the service is
- who it is for
- age groups
- programs offered
- location
- class format
- pricing
- trial information
- contact methods
- frequently asked questions

Use descriptive headings.

Avoid vague marketing text when a concrete factual answer can be provided.

## 13. Structured data

If structured data already exists, preserve and extend it rather than replacing it unnecessarily.

Structured data must match visible page content.

Do not fabricate:

- reviews
- ratings
- addresses
- prices
- business claims
- awards
- certifications

Do not add schema properties that are not supported by actual website content or reliable project data.

## 14. FAQ implementation

When creating or updating FAQ content:

- make the question explicit
- provide a direct answer immediately
- keep answers understandable without additional context
- avoid keyword stuffing
- avoid duplicate questions with slightly different wording
- reuse factual information already present on the site
- keep FAQ answers consistent with other pages

If FAQ structured data is used, it must correspond to visible FAQ content.

## 15. Accessibility

Preserve or improve accessibility.

When relevant:

- images should have meaningful alt text
- decorative images should use appropriate empty alt text
- buttons should be actual buttons when they perform actions
- links should have understandable labels
- forms should have labels
- keyboard interaction should remain functional
- text should remain readable on mobile
- interactive elements should have sufficient context

Do not sacrifice accessibility for visual effects.

## 16. Performance

Keep the site lightweight.

Avoid unnecessarily large:

- JavaScript bundles
- CSS files
- images
- fonts
- third-party scripts

Do not introduce heavy libraries for small UI effects.

Preserve lazy loading and image optimization when already present.

## 17. Mobile-first awareness

Many visitors may use phones.

When modifying a page, check that the change does not create:

- horizontal scrolling
- overflowing text
- oversized buttons
- unreadable text
- broken navigation
- overlapping sections
- excessively large images
- unusable tap targets

## 18. Code comments

All code comments must be written in English.

Do not add Chinese comments to source code.

Use UTF-8 encoding.

## 19. No emojis in code

Do not use emojis in:

- HTML comments
- CSS comments
- JavaScript comments
- JavaScript logs
- configuration files
- technical documentation generated for the repository

Visible website content may only use emojis if explicitly requested.

## 20. Root cause over fallback

If something is broken, investigate the cause first.

Do not automatically add fallback logic.

For example, do not solve:

missing data -> display arbitrary default value

without first determining why the expected data is missing.

Prefer fixing the source condition.

## 21. Avoid unnecessary legacy compatibility

Do not add compatibility code for old implementations, old browsers, deprecated APIs, or abandoned layouts unless there is evidence that the project still requires them.

Prefer keeping the current codebase simple.

## 22. Verify affected references

After changing a page, component, ID, class, or URL, search the repository for references to it.

Check related:

- HTML files
- CSS selectors
- JavaScript selectors
- internal links
- navigation
- structured data
- sitemap
- robots.txt
- metadata

Do not assume a file or selector is used in only one location.

## 23. Do not invent project information

Never invent project facts.

If information such as the following is not present in the repository or user instructions, do not guess:

- prices
- schedules
- age ranges
- addresses
- phone numbers
- emails
- reviews
- ratings
- class sizes
- program names
- business claims

Ask or inspect the repository instead.

## 24. Completion standard

Before considering a requested change complete:

1. verify the requested feature exists
2. inspect modified files
3. check for broken references
4. check for obvious HTML, CSS, or JavaScript errors
5. verify GitHub Pages compatibility
6. consider desktop and mobile behavior
7. verify important SEO or structured-data content was not accidentally removed

Do not report completion before performing reasonable verification.